Title:
Use-After-Free - HEVD (+ SMEP/KASLR/KPP Bypass)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This write-up walks through exploiting a non-paged pool Use-After-Free in HackSysExtremeVulnerableDriver (HEVD) on Windows 10 1809, from source review to a working privilege escalation. It explains how the driver leaves a dangling global pointer after freeing an object containing a function pointer callback, enabling control-flow hijack via pool reclaiming/spraying (notably using named pipes/IOCTL-driven allocations). The post demonstrates bypassing SMEP by redirecting execution to a kernel gadget (`add al, 0x10; call rax`) found in `cng.sys`, allowing execution of attacker-controlled code placed in reclaimed pool memory. It also covers practical kernel exploitation pitfalls like GS cookie/stack alignment issues and KASLR bypass via leaking loaded driver bases with `EnumDeviceDrivers`. For privilege escalation, it uses a “data-only” style technique by flipping `_KTHREAD.PreviousMode` to `KernelMode` to make `NtReadVirtualMemory`/`NtWriteVirtualMemory` usable for arbitrary kernel memory access, then performs EPROCESS token stealing and restores state to reduce PatchGuard/KPP impact. Useful for red teamers and exploit dev learners focusing on Windows kernel pool exploitation and mitigation bypass patterns.

Technical Focus:
- HEVD non-paged pool Use-After-Free (dangling function pointer callback)
- Pool reclaiming/spraying (named pipes, pool tags, allocation sizing)
- SMEP bypass via kernel gadget/ret2kernel trampoline (cng.sys gadget)
- KASLR bypass via driver base address leakage (EnumDeviceDrivers / Psapi)
- GS cookie / stack alignment to avoid bugchecks during kernel callback return
- PreviousMode corruption in `_KTHREAD` enabling syscall-based kernel R/W + token stealing

Use Cases:
- Reproducing and learning Windows kernel UAF exploitation in a controlled lab (HEVD)
- Developing SMEP-aware kernel payload staging using in-kernel gadgets
- Practicing pool reclaiming strategies and debugging with WinDBG/DbgPrint
- Studying PreviousMode-based privilege escalation primitives and PatchGuard considerations
- Building detection ideas around suspicious pool sprays, driver base enumeration, and PreviousMode tampering

Keywords:
HEVD, Use-After-Free, NonPagedPool, ExAllocatePoolWithTag, ExFreePoolWithTag, IOCTL, METHOD_NEITHER, pool reclaiming, named pipes, WinDBG, SMEP, CR4, KASLR, PatchGuard, KPP, GS cookie, stack alignment, ropper, cng.sys, gadget, EnumDeviceDrivers, GetDeviceDriverBaseName, ntoskrnl.exe, PsInitialSystemProcess, EPROCESS, token stealing, _KTHREAD, PreviousMode, NtReadVirtualMemory, NtWriteVirtualMemory